WEBVTT

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:19.420
Isolate the harm first. A finance workstation is rapidly changing
shared files, and a ransom note appears. If encryption is
spreading, prioritize isolating affected systems through your
incident-response process. Do not wait for perfect attribution
while damage continues.

00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:31.847
Preserve logs and feasible volatile evidence in parallel, and
coordinate with the incident lead and system owners. Consider
business and safety dependencies when selecting the containment
method.

00:00:33.593 --> 00:00:48.593
Power-off has an evidence tradeoff. Shutting a machine down may
destroy information held in memory. Prefer network isolation when
available. If isolation and disconnection are unavailable, power-
down may be necessary to limit further damage.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:01:06.820
Next, scope affected systems, identities, shared storage, and
backup access. Investigate whether compromise began earlier and
whether data was stolen. Encryption and data theft are separate
questions. Missing alerts do not prove that no data left.

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:26.487
Recovery needs proof. A successful backup job is not a successful
restore test. Validate an appropriately protected backup in an
isolated environment, address the entry point and persistence,
remediate affected credentials, and verify essential business
functions before reconnecting restored systems.

00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:44.753
In an interview, distinguish containment, eradication, and
recovery. Explain what has been confirmed, what remains uncertain,
and who owns the next decision. The goal is not merely to bring
systems back online. It is to restore trustworthy service.
